267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 212 0 R
/E (Through initiatives such as our End Period Poverty Project, we have witnessed both the challenges faced by women and girls and the extraordinary resilience they demonstrate every day. We have learned that sustainable change requires more than providing menstrual products. It requires education, advocacy, supportive policies, improved infrastructure, community engagement, and innovations that make menstrual care accessible, affordable, and environmentally sustainable.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 212 0 R
/E (The State of Menstrual Equity in Africa 2026 was developed to shine a light on these realities while also celebrating the remarkable progress being made across the continent. It brings together evidence, research, and lived experiences to provide a comprehensive picture of menstrual health in Africa today. More importantly, it reminds us that behind every statistic is a girl with dreams, a young woman pursuing an education, a mother supporting her family, or a community striving for a better future.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 212 0 R
/E (At A Thousand Women Africa, we believe that menstrual equity is fundamental to achieving gender equality, improving educational outcomes, advancing public health, and unlocking economic opportunity. When girls can manage their periods safely and with dignity, they are more likely to remain in school, participate confidently in society, and realise their full potential. Investing in menstrual health is therefore an investment )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
270 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 212 0 R
/E (in Africa's future.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
271 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 212 0 R
/E (Every generation is presented with defining challenges that test its commitment to equality, dignity, and justice. For ours, one of those challenges is ensuring that no woman or girl is disadvantaged simply because she menstruates.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
272 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 212 0 R
/E (Menstruation is a natural part of life. Yet for millions of women and girls across Africa, it remains surrounded by stigma, silence, and systemic barriers. Too many girls continue to miss school because they cannot afford menstrual products. Too many women navigate workplaces and public spaces without adequate facilities. Too many communities still lack access to accurate menstrual health education, safe sanitation, and quality healthcare.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
273 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 212 0 R
/E (These realities are not inevitable. They are the result of inequalities that can and must be addressed.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
