1021 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 252 0 R
/E (The statistics presented throughout this chapter reveal a clear reality: menstrual inequity remains a significant challenge affecting millions of women and girls across Africa. Behind every percentage is a person whose education, health, confidence, and opportunities may be shaped by circumstances beyond their control.)
/Pg 52 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1022 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 252 0 R
/E (Yet these statistics tell only part of the story.)
/Pg 52 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1023 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 252 0 R
/E (Across the continent, women and girls continue to demonstrate remarkable resilience, while governments, civil society organisations, researchers, entrepreneurs, and communities are increasingly recognising menstrual health as a critical development priority. Progress is being made through policy reforms, improved education, local innovation, and advocacy, but significant gaps remain.)
/Pg 52 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1024 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 252 0 R
/E (The evidence also highlights an important truth: menstrual inequity is not caused by menstruation itself. It is the result of systemic barriers, including poverty, inadequate sanitation, limited healthcare, persistent stigma, and insufficient investment. These barriers are preventable, and with coordinated action, they are solvable.)
/Pg 52 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1025 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 252 0 R
/E (For Africa, investing in menstrual equity is not simply about providing menstrual products. It is about investing in girls' education, protecting women's health, advancing gender equality, strengthening economic participation, and upholding human dignity.)
/Pg 52 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1026 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 252 0 R
/E (Organisations such as A Thousand Women Africa demonstrate how locally led initiatives can contribute to lasting change by combining menstrual product access with education, advocacy, community engagement, and sustainable solutions. Likewise, innovations such as Luwa reflect the growing potential for African-designed products and social enterprises to make menstrual care more accessible, affordable, and environmentally sustainable.)
/Pg 52 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1027 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 252 0 R
/E (As Africa's youthful population continues to grow, the decisions made today will shape the experiences of future generations. Every school equipped with safe sanitation facilities, every community that challenges menstrual stigma, every government that invests in menstrual health, and every organisation that champions menstrual equity contributes to a future where no girl is excluded because of a natural biological process.)
/Pg 52 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
